#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22f462bf-af92-5f66-b161-1437e27ac16d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283812} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1e8475-4581-5d54-82c8-30a79f5b84c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283813} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd23f33-7e15-58a1-8b3c-cfba4dd114bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283814} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa270f1-39b7-5559-91a0-c3ac999965f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283815} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd5b2c0-51ad-5626-9b43-54b1a0d7fccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f1e8475-4581-5d54-82c8-30a79f5b84c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283813} :answer-field/id #uuid "cca04b2b-a4de-5524-b216-2806fb864de7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41a5decc-6a63-5e15-9219-32df767b7987" :question/math-academy-id "q-127821" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=-2\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 6=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(A)=0,$ we conclude that $A$ is not invertible. Since $A$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $A$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(C)=1\\cdot 5-7\\cdot 0=5.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(C)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is singular."}]}
